The '''AP NFL Comeback Player of the Year (CBPOY)''' is an annual award presented by the [[Associated Press]] (AP) to a player in the [[National Football League]] (NFL). While the criteria for the award is imprecise,<ref>{{cite magazine|last1=Burke|first1=Chris|title=Why Melvin Gordon deserves to win NFL's Comeback Player of the Year award|url=https://www.si.com/nfl/2016/11/07/melvin-gordon-san-diego-chargers-comeback-player-of-the-year|access-date=March 20, 2017|magazine=Sports Illustrated|date=November 7, 2016|quote=This is a good spot for a reminder that the Comeback Player of the Year criteria is, well, not exactly precise.|archive-date=March 21, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170321165829/http://www.si.com/nfl/2016/11/07/melvin-gordon-san-diego-chargers-comeback-player-of-the-year|url-status=live}}</ref> it is typically given to a player who overcomes adversity to return to play.<ref>{{cite news|title=NFL Comeback Player of the Year Award |url=http://www.football-almanac.com/awards/NFL_Comeback_Player_of_the_Year_Award.shtml |access-date=March 20, 2017 |publisher=Football Almanac |url-status=bot: unknown |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100113081439/http://www.football-almanac.com/awards/NFL_Comeback_Player_of_the_Year_Award.shtml |archive-date=January 13, 2010 }}</ref> The winner is selected by a nationwide panel of media personnel.<ref>{{cite news |title=NFL Comeback Player of the Year |url=http://www.sandiegouniontribune.com/sdut-nfl-comeback-player-of-the-year-2014feb01-story.html |date=February 1, 2014 |work=[[The San Diego Union-Tribune]] |access-date=March 20, 2017 |agency=[[Associated Press]] |archive-date=March 21, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170321171609/http://www.sandiegouniontribune.com/sdut-nfl-comeback-player-of-the-year-2014feb01-story.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Since 2011, the award has been presented at the [[NFL Honors]] ceremony held in the days leading up to the [[Super Bowl]]. Beginning with the 2024 season, the criteria for the award was changed to emphasize players coming back from "illness, physical injury, or other circumstances that led him to miss playing time the previous season", as opposed to players having resurgent performances or "coming back from sucking".<ref>{{cite web|last=Dubin|first=Jared|url=https://www.cbssports.com/nfl/news/nfl-comeback-player-of-the-year-award-criteria-clarified-to-emphasize-injury-illness-after-joe-flaccos-win/|title= NFL Comeback Player of the Year award criteria clarified to emphasize injury, illness after Joe Flacco's win |work=[[CBS Sports]]|date=June 19, 2024|accessdate=January 6, 2025}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Florio|first=Mike|author-link=Mike Florio|url=https://www.nbcsports.com/nfl/profootballtalk/rumor-mill/news/new-comeback-player-of-the-year-guidelines-could-frustrate-some-gamblers|title=New Comeback Player of the Year guidelines could frustrate some gamblers|publisher=[[NBC Sports]]|work=[[Pro Football Talk]]|date=June 19, 2024|accessdate=January 6, 2025}}</ref>

The AP first recognized an NFL comeback player of the year from 1963 to 1966, but these players are typically not included in overall lists of winners.<ref>{{cite book|title=2015 Official NFL Record and Fact Book|date=2015|publisher=National Football League|page=527|url=https://nflcommunications.com/Documents/2015%20NFL%20Record%20and%20Fact%20Book.pdf|access-date=August 1, 2016|editor1-last=Lee|editor1-first=Brenden|editor2-last=Gellerman|editor2-first=Jacob|editor3-last=King|editor3-first=Robert|archive-date=October 1, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151001192546/http://www.nfl.com/static/content/public/photo/2015/07/21/0ap3000000502939.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref> The AP did not give the award again until the 1998 season. Only two players have received the award more than once since the [[AFL–NFL merger]]: quarterbacks [[Chad Pennington]] (2006 and 2008) and [[Joe Burrow]] (2021 and 2024).<ref>{{Cite news|last=Rolfe|first=Ben|date=February 7, 2021|title=2020 NFL Comeback Player of the Year: Alex Smith, Washington Football Team|language=en-US|website=Pro Football Network|url=https://www.profootballnetwork.com/who-won-2020-nfl-comeback-player-of-the-year-award/|access-date=February 20, 2021|quote=Since the award’s reinstatement in 1998, only Chad Pennington has won the award twice. However, the award was also briefly awarded by the Associated Press between 1963 and 1966. During that period, two awards were given each year. One for the NFL, and one for the AFL. During that time, RB Paul Lowe of the Chargers was also named Comeback Player of the Year twice (1963 and 1965).|archive-date=February 7, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210207030847/https://www.profootballnetwork.com/who-won-2020-nfl-comeback-player-of-the-year-award/|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:ChadPennington1-crop.jpg|thumb|[[Chad Pennington]] (''pictured'') along with [[Joe Burrow]] each won two Comeback Player of the Year awards, the most in NFL history.]]

